12l-96 320A21 21 0 0 1 208 437z"></path></svg> <span>Programs</span> </a><a class="custom-nav-node" href="php-oop" title="PHP OOP"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1em" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 16 16" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M6 3.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 7.5 2h1A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 10 3.5v1A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 8.5 6v1H11a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v1a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0V8h-5v.5a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-1A.5.5 0 0 1 5 7h2.5V6A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 6 4.5zM8.5 5a.5.5 0 0 0 .5-.5v-1a.5.5 0 0 0-.5-.5h-1a.5.5 0 0 0-.5.5v1a.5.5 0 0 0 .5.5zM3 11.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 4.5 10h1A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 7 11.5v1A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 5.5 14h-1A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 3 12.5zm1.5-.5a.5.5 0 0 0-.5.5v1a.5.5 0 0 0 .5.5h1a.5.5 0 0 0 .5-.5v-1a.5.5 0 0 0-.5-.5zm4.5.5a1.5 1.5 0 0 1 1.5-1.5h1a1.5 1.5 0 0 1 1.5 1.5v1a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5h-1A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 9 12.5zm1.5-.5a.5.5 0 0 0-.5.5v1a.5.5 0 0 0 .5.5h1a.5.5 0 0 0 .5-.5v-1a.5.5 0 0 0-.5-.5z" fill-rule="evenodd"></path></svg> <span>OOPs</span> </a><a class="custom-nav-node" href="php-functions" title="PHP Functions"><svg baseprofile="tiny" fill="currentColor" height="1em" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" version="1.2" viewbox="0 0 24 24" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M17 16c-1.305 0-2.403.837-2.816 2h-3.184c-1.654 0-3-1.346-3-3v-3.025c.838.634 1.87 1.025 3 1.025h3.184c.413 1.163 1.512 2 2.816 2 1.657 0 3-1.343 3-3s-1.343-3-3-3c-1.305 0-2.403.837-2.816 2h-3.184c-1.654 0-3-1.346-3-3v-.184c1.163-.413 2-1.512 2-2.816 0-1.657-1.343-3-3-3s-3 1.343-3 3c0 1.304.837 2.403 2 2.816v7.184c0 2.757 2.243 5 5 5h3.184c.413 1.163 1.512 2 2.816 2 1.657 0 3-1.343 3-3s-1.343-3-3-3zm0-5c.552 0 1 .449 1 1s-.448 1-1 1-1-.449-1-1 .448-1 1-1zm-10-7c.552 0 1 .449 1 1s-.448 1-1 1-1-.449-1-1 .448-1 1-1zm10 16c-.552 0-1-.449-1-1s.448-1 1-1 1 .449 1 1-.448 1-1 1z"></path></svg><span>Functions</span> </a><a class="custom-nav-node custom-nav-node--active" href="php-interview-questions" title="PHP Interview Questions"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1em" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M256 8C119.043 8 8 119.083 8 256c0 136.997 111.043 248 248 248s248-111.003 248-248C504 119.083 392.957 8 256 8zm0 448c-110.532 0-200-89.431-200-200 0-110.495 89.472-200 200-200 110.491 0 200 89.471 200 200 0 110.53-89.431 200-200 200zm107.244-255.2c0 67.052-72.421 68.084-72.421 92.863V300c0 6.627-5.373 12-12 12h-45.647c-6.627 0-12-5.373-12-12v-8.659c0-35.745 27.1-50.034 47.579-61.516 17.561-9.845 28.324-16.541 28.324-29.579 0-17.246-21.999-28.693-39.784-28.693-23.189 0-33.894 10.977-48.942 29.969-4.057 5.12-11.46 6.071-16.666 2.124l-27.824-21.098c-5.107-3.872-6.251-11.066-2.644-16.363C184.846 131.491 214.94 112 261.794 112c49.071 0 101.45 38.304 101.45 88.8zM298 368c0 23.159-18.841 42-42 42s-42-18.841-42-42 18.841-42 42-42 42 18.841 42 42z"></path></svg> <span>Interview Questions</span></a></div></div><h1 class="h1">PHP Interview Questions and Answers</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>4 Jun 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 18 min read</span></div><img alt="PHP Interview Questions" class="imageright" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/interview/images/php-interview-questions.png"><p>There is given PHP interview questions and answers that have been asked in many companies. Let's see the list of top PHP interview questions.</p><h3 class="h3">1) What is PHP?</h3><p>PHP stands for <em>Hypertext Preprocessor</em>. It is an open-source server-side scripting language which is widely used for web development. It supports many databases like MySQL, Oracle, Sybase, Solid, PostgreSQL, generic ODBC etc.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-tutorial">PHP Tutorial</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">2) What is PEAR in PHP?</h3><p>PEAR is a framework and <em>repository for reusable PHP components</em>. PEAR stands for PHP Extension and Application Repository. It contains all types of PHP code snippets and libraries. It also provides a command line interface to install "packages" automatically.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-pear">PHP PEAR</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">3) Who is known as the father of PHP?</h3><p><em>Rasmus Lerdorf</em> is recognised as the creator of PHP, having used it in 1994 to manage how many people were viewing his website.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">4) What was the old name of PHP?</h3><p>PHP originally stood for "Personal Home Pages", indicating its use for building personal sites.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">5) Explain the difference b/w static and dynamic websites.</h3><p>On a static website, everyone visiting the site gets the same information because there is no automatic change to the HTML. Such sites require less time and effort than others, though they are basic and lack active elements.</p><p>With a dynamic website, the server uses scripts (such as PHP) to make the content as it is requested. It can show content that's made just for you, get information from databases, and react when people type or click-all of which makes it great for things like websites, blogs, and online shopping sites.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">6) What is the Name of the Scripting Engine in PHP?</h3><p>The PHP scripting engine is known as the Zend Engine. Since PHP 7, Zend Engine 3 has been introduced, which helps the language run faster and consume less memory than it did with Zend Engine 2 in PHP 5.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">7) What were the key changes introduced with new PHP versions?</h3><p><strong>PHP 4</strong>: With PHP 4, the Zend Engine 1 was first provided, yet there was little support for using OOP.</p><p><strong>PHP 5</strong>: Introduced classes and exceptions and allowed the use of PDO to make database access easier. It runs on Zend Engine 2.</p><p><strong>PHP 7</strong>: With Zend Engine 3, PHP 7 can work faster, permits mentioning the types of scalars, has new return types, the null coalescing operator (??) and anonymous classes.</p><p><strong>PHP 8</strong>: Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation, union types, named argument functionality, support for attributes (annotations) and match expressions were introduced to improve performance and make the language more flexible.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">8) What are the Popular Content Management Systems (CMS) in PHP?</h3><p><strong>WordPress:</strong> WordPress is a free and open-source content management system (CMS) based on PHP &amp; MySQL. It includes a plug-in architecture and template system. It is mostly connected with blogging but supports another kind of web content, containing more traditional mailing lists and forums, media displays, and online stores.</p><p><strong>Joomla:</strong> Joomla is a free and open-source content management system (CMS) for distributing web content created by Open Source Matters, Inc. It is based on a model-view-controller web application framework that can be used independently of the CMS.</p><p><strong>Magento:</strong> Magento is an open source E-trade programming, made by Varien Inc., which is valuable for online business. It has a flexible measured design and is versatile, with many control alternatives that are useful for clients. Magento utilises E-trade stage which offers organisation extreme E-business arrangements and extensive support network.</p><p><strong>Drupal:</strong> Drupal is a CMS platform developed in PHP and distributed under the GNU (General Public License).</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">9) What are the Popular Frameworks in PHP?</h3><p><strong>CakePHP:</strong> This is a fast framework that applies the MVC pattern, making the process of building web applications easier by providing helpful built-in tools.</p><p><strong>CodeIgniter:</strong> This framework is small, straightforward to pick up, and a good option for newbies.</p><p><strong>Yii 2:</strong> A high-performance framework that uses smaller pieces to help build big apps and comes with useful tools like the Gii code generator and good security.</p><p><strong>Symfony:</strong> A well-made system used for big projects, giving access to reusable tools and a wide selection of support.</p><p><strong>Zend Framework (now Laminas):</strong> This framework is built with an emphasis on planning enterprise apps and stressing that features and standards are extensible for professional developers.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-frameworks">PHP Frameworks</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">10) What are the Characteristics of PHP?</h3><p>PHP is used on the server side to create web applications. Here are the main features it displays:</p><ul class="points"><li>Open-source and free to use.</li><li>You can use it on Windows, Linux, and macOS.</li><li>Its syntax is much like that of C and Perl, meaning programmers who use them will find it easier to use PHP.</li><li>Can embed directly into HTML.</li><li>Almost all Python frameworks come with support for MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQLite.</li><li>Using JavaScript makes websites responsive and popular among developers.</li></ul><p>To Read More: <a href="features-of-php">Features of PHP</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">11) List some features of PHP 8, and compare them to PHP 7.</h3><p>There are several improvements in PHP 8 compared to PHP 7. We have summarised each feature and the main differences, as listed below:</p><table class="alt"><tbody><tr><th>Feature</th><th>PHP 8</th><th>PHP 7</th></tr><tr><td><strong>JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler</strong></td><td>Raises performance by generating an optimised program during program execution.</td><td>No JIT support; the only way to run Python codes is with a traditional interpreter.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Named arguments</strong></td><td>Supporting argument passing by name provides better readability.</td><td>Positional arguments were the only ones that were supported.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Attributes (annotations)</strong></td><td>Allows for attaching metadata to classes and functions directly using #[Attr].</td><td>Code in PHP requires specific comments or the use of libraries.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Union types</strong></td><td>It is now possible to use different types (`int` and `float`) as inputs in a function.</td><td>&lt;text&gt;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Promoting Constructor properties</strong></td><td>Sets and creates properties in the constructor where the object is created.</td><td>Each Mapping Object must include the mandatory assignment of a property.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nullsafe operator (?-&gt;)</strong></td><td>Way to read object properties or access object methods securely without explicit null check.</td><td>Necessary nested if statements should be used for null checks.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Match expression</strong></td><td>A comparison and return value version of the enhanced switch statement.</td><td>Change the name of a variable only when it is used for loose comparison.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Improved type errors</strong></td><td>Clearer and easier-to-understand error messages.</td><td>Handling commitment or query problems sometimes lacks the necessary detail.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In addition to what PHP 7 offers, the newest version also makes development easier, resolves errors more effectively and offers new features for modern systems.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">12) What is "echo" in PHP?</h3><p>PHP echo outputs one or more strings. It is a language construct, not a function. So, the use of parentheses is not required. But if you want to pass more than one parameter to echo, the use of parentheses is required.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">void echo ( string $arg1 [, string $... ] )</textarea></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-echo">PHP Echo</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">13) What is "print" in PHP?</h3><p>PHP print outputs a string. It is a language construct, not a function. So the use of parentheses is not required with the argument list. Unlike echo, it always returns 1.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">int print ( string $arg)</textarea></div><p>To Read More: <a href="php-print">PHP Print</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">14) What is the difference between "echo" and "print" in PHP?</h3><p><strong>Echo</strong> can <em>output one or more string</em>s, but <strong>print</strong> can only <em>output one string and always returns 1</em>.</p><p><strong>Echo</strong> is <em>faster than print</em> because it does not return any value.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">15) How a variable is declared in PHP?</h3><p>A PHP variable is the name of the memory location that holds data. It is temporary storage.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">$variableName=value;</textarea></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-variables">PHP Variables</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">16) What is the difference between $message and $$message?</h3><p><strong>$message</strong> <em>stores variable data</em> while <strong>$$message</strong> is used <em>to store variable variables</em>.</p><p>$message stores fixed data, whereas the data stored in $$message may be changed dynamically.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-dollar-doubledollar">PHP $ and $$</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">17) What are the ways to define a constant in PHP?</h3><p>PHP constants are names or identifiers that can't be changed during the execution of the script. PHP constants are defined in two ways:</p><ul class="points"><li>Using define() function</li><li>Using const() function</li></ul><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-constants">PHP Constants</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">18) What are magic constants in PHP?</h3><p>PHP magic constants are predefined constants that change based on their use. They start with a double underscore (__) and end with a double underscore (__).</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-magic-constants">PHP Magic Constants</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">19) How many data types are there in PHP?</h3><p>PHP data types are used to hold different types of data or values. There are eight primitive data types, which are further categorised into three types:</p><ul class="points"><li>Scalar types</li><li>Compound types</li><li>Special types</li></ul><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-data-types">PHP Data Types</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">20) How to do single and multi-line comments in PHP?</h3><p>PHP single-line comment is made in two ways:</p><ul class="points"><li>Using // (C++ style single line comment)</li><li>Using # (Unix Shell style single line comment)</li></ul><p>PHP multi-line comment is made by enclosing all lines within.</p><p>To Read More: <a href="php-comments">PHP Comments</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">21) What are the different loops in PHP?</h3><p>The <strong>for, while, do-while </strong>and<strong> foreach</strong> loops are used in PHP to execute the following lines of code repeatedly, depending on certain conditions or a bunch of elements.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">22) What is the use of the count() function in PHP?</h3><p>The PHP count() function is used <em>to count the total elements in the array or something an object</em>.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">23) What is the use of the header() function in PHP?</h3><p>The header() function is used to send a raw HTTP header to a client. It must be called before sending the actual output. For example, you can't print any HTML element before using this function.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">24) What does isset() function?</h3><p>The isset() function checks if the variable is defined and not null.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">25) Explain PHP parameterised functions.</h3><p>Parameterised functions allow the same function to respond to different inputs by getting values passed to them (parameters) when it is called.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">function greet($name) {
    echo "Hello, $name!";
}
greet("Alice");
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre>Hello, Alice!</pre></div><p>This allows for code reuse and flexibility by letting the function behave differently based on the arguments passed to it.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-parameterized-function">PHP Parameterized Function</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">26) Explain the PHP variable length argument function</h3><p>PHP supports the variable length argument function. It means you can pass 0, 1 or n number of arguments in a function. To do this, you need to use three ellipses (dots) before the argument name. The three-dot concept has been implemented for variable-length arguments since PHP 5.6.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-variable-length-argument-function">PHP Variable Length Argument Function</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">27) What are superglobals in PHP?</h3><p>Superglobals are always present in PHP and you can use them from any scope. Examples include:</p><p><strong>$_GET</strong>: data from URL parameters</p><p><strong>$_POST</strong>: data from form submissions</p><p><strong>$_SESSION</strong>: session variables</p><p><strong>$_COOKIE</strong>: cookies</p><p><strong>$_SERVER</strong>: server and execution environment info</p><p>They simplify data handling across your PHP application.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">28) What is the array in PHP?</h3><p>An array is used to store multiple values in a single value. In PHP, it orders maps of pairs of keys and values. It saves the collection of the data type.</p><p>To Read More: <a href="php-array">PHP Array</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">29) How many types of arrays are there in PHP?</h3><p>There are three types of arrays in PHP:</p><ol class="points"><li><strong>Indexed array:</strong>an array with a numeric key.</li><li><strong>Associative array:</strong>an array where each key has its specific value.</li><li><strong>Multidimensional array:</strong>an array containing one or more arrays within itself.</li></ol><hr/><h3 class="h3">30) Explain some of the PHP array functions.</h3><p>Array functions in PHP are as follows:</p><p><strong>array()</strong>: Creates a new array.</p><p><strong>array_change_key_case()</strong>: Changes the case of all keys in an array to upper or lower case.</p><p><strong>array_chunk()</strong>: Splits an array into smaller chunks of a specified size.</p><p><strong>count()</strong>: Returns the number of elements in an array.</p><p><strong>sort()</strong>: Sorts an indexed array in ascending order, reindexing the array.</p><p><strong>array_reverse()</strong>: Returns an array with elements in reverse order.</p><p><strong>array_search()</strong>: Searches for a value in an array and returns its key if found.</p><p><strong>array_intersect()</strong>: Compares two or more arrays and returns the values that are common to all.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-array-functions">PHP Array Functions</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">31) What is the difference between an indexed and associative array?</h3><p>The indexed array holds elements in an indexed form, which is represented by a number starting from 0 and incremented by 1. For example:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">$season=&lt;strong&gt;array&lt;/strong&gt;("summer", "winter", "spring", "autumn");</textarea></div><p>The associative array holds elements with names. For example:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">$salary=&lt;strong&gt;array&lt;/strong&gt;("Sonoo"=&gt;"350000","John"=&gt;"450000","Kartik"=&gt;"200000");</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">32) How to get the length of a string in PHP?</h3><p>The strlen() function helps count every single character, no matter if they are alphanumeric or not, in a string.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">strlen($string)</textarea></div><p><strong>Example</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">echo strlen("Hello World");</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre>11</pre></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-string-functions">PHP String Functions</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">33) Explain some of the PHP string functions.</h3><p>The string functions of PHP are as follows:</p><p><strong>strtolower()</strong>: Converts all characters in a string to lowercase.</p><p><strong>strtoupper()</strong>: Converts all characters in a string to uppercase.</p><p><strong>ucfirst()</strong>: Capitalises the first character of a string.</p><p><strong>lcfirst()</strong>: Converts the first character of a string to lowercase.</p><p><strong>ucwords()</strong>: Capitalises the first character of each word in a string.</p><p><strong>strrev()</strong>: Reverses the string.</p><p><strong>strlen()</strong>: Returns the length of the string (number of characters).</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">34) What are the methods to submit forms in PHP?</h3><p>PHP provides two ways to send a form to the server: via GET or POST. They decide on the procedure for sending form data to the server.</p><p>To Read More: <a href="php-form">PHP Form</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">35) How can you submit a form without a submit button?</h3><p>It is possible to submit a form merely by adding JavaScript's submit() method to it. As a result, data can be sent automatically by the form when certain events occur, rather than having to click a button.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">36) What are the ways to include files in PHP?</h3><p>PHP allows you to include files so that page content can be reused again. There are two ways to add the file in PHP.</p><ol class="points"><li>include</li><li>require</li></ol><p>To Read More: <a href="php-include-file">PHP Include File</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">37) Differentiate between require and include?</h3><p><em>require</em> and <em>include</em> both are used to include a file, but if data is not found, <em>include sends a warning</em>, whereas <em>require sends a fatal error</em>.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">38) Explain setcookie() function in PHP?</h3><p>PHP setcookie() function is used to set cookies with HTTP response. Once the cookie is set, you can access it by the $_COOKIE superglobal variable.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">bool setcookie ( string $name [, string $value [, int $expire = 0 [, string $path
[, string $domain [, bool $secure = false [, bool $httponly = false ]]]]]] )</textarea></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-cookie">PHP Cookie</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">39) How can you retrieve a cookie value?</h3><p>PHP allows you to get a cookie value using the $_COOKIE superglobal. For example:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">echo $_COOKIE ["user"];</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">40) What is a session?</h3><p>PHP Engine creates a logical object to preserve data across subsequent HTTP requests, which is known as a session.</p><p>Sessions generally store temporary data to allow multiple PHP pages to offer a complete functional transaction for the same user.</p><p>Simply, it maintains the data of a user (browser).</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-session">PHP Session</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">41) What is session handling in PHP?</h3><p>With session handling, you can save the user's information for each page you load. PHP does this through a special session ID saved in a user's browser.</p><p><strong>Example</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">session_start(); // To start the session
$_SESSION["username"] = "john_doe"; // To set a session variable</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">42) What is $_SESSION in PHP?</h3><p>$_SESSION in PHP allows for data to be stored across various pages by one user. Since session data is held on the server, it is more secure than cookies.</p><p>You should call session_start() at the beginning of your script to use $_SESSION, as follows:</p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">session_start();
$_SESSION["username"] = "prachetyerr"
echo $_SESSION["username"]; 
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre>prachetyerr</pre></div><p>Sessions are often employed for validating users, looking after their shopping cart items and saving their choices during a session.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">43) What is PHP session_start() and session_destroy() function?</h3><p>PHP session_start() function is used to start the session. It starts new or resumes the current session. It returns the current session if the session is created already. If the session is not available, it creates and returns new sessions.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">44) What is the difference between a session and a cookie?</h3><p>The main difference between session and cookie is that <em>cookies are stored on the user's computer in the text file</em> format while <em>sessions are stored on the server side</em>.</p><p>Cookies can't hold multiple variables; on the other hand, a Session can hold multiple variables.</p><p>You can manually set an expiry for a cookie, while the session only remains active as long as the browser is open.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">45) Write syntax to open a file in PHP.</h3><p>PHP fopen() function is used to open files or URLs and returns resources. It accepts two arguments: $filename and $mode.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">resource fopen ( string $filename , string $mode [, bool $use_include_path = false [, resource $context ]] )</textarea></div><p>To Read More: <a href="php-open-file">PHP Open File</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">46) How to read a file in PHP?</h3><p>PHP provides various functions to read data from the file. Different functions allow you to read all file data, read data line by line, and read data character by character.</p><p>PHP file read functions are given below:</p><ul class="points"><li>fread()</li><li>fgets()</li><li>fgetc()</li></ul><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-read-file">PHP Read File</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">47) How to write in a file in PHP?</h3><p>PHP fwrite() and fputs() functions are used to write data into the file. To write data into a file, you need to use w, r+, w+, x, x+, c or c+ mode.</p><p>To Read More: <a href="php-write-file">PHP Write File</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">48) How to delete files in PHP?</h3><p>The unlink() function is used to delete a file in PHP.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">bool unlink (string $filename)</textarea></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-delete-file">PHP Delete File</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">49) What is the method to execute a PHP script from the command line?</h3><p>You should just run the PHP command line interface (CLI) and specify the file name of the script to be executed as follows.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">50) How to upload files in PHP?</h3><p>The move_uploaded_file() function is used to upload files in PHP.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">bool move_uploaded_file ( string $filename , string $destination )</textarea></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-file-upload">PHP File Upload</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">51) How to download files in PHP?</h3><p>The readfile() function is used to download the file in PHP.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">int readfile ( string $filename )</textarea></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-download-file">PHP Download File</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">52) How can you send email in PHP?</h3><p>The mail() function is used to send email in PHP.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">bool mail($to,$subject,$message,$header);</textarea></div><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-mail">PHP Mail</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">53) How do you connect MySQL database with PHP?</h3><p>There are two methods to connect MySQL database with PHP: Procedural and object-oriented style.</p><p><strong>Procedural style:</strong> Requires the use of functions such as mysqli_connect() to create a link. It's not complicated and functions well for uncomplicated scripts.</p><p><strong>Object-oriented style:</strong> Establishes a database connection using new mysqli(). It looks better and is preferred for applications with many parts.</p><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-mysql-connect">PHP MySQL Connect</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">54) How to create a connection in PHP?</h3><p>The mysqli_connect() function is used to create a connection in PHP.</p><p><strong>Syntax</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">resource mysqli_connect (server, username, password)</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">55) How to create a database connection and query in PHP?</h3><p>Since PHP 4.3, mysql_reate_db() is deprecated. Now, you can use the following two alternatives.</p><ul class="points"><li>mysqli_query()</li><li>PDO::_query()</li></ul><p><strong>To Read More:</strong> <a href="php-mysql-create">PHP MySQL Create</a></p><hr/><h3 class="h3">56) How can we increase the execution time of a PHP script?</h3><p>By default, the maximum execution time for PHP scripts is set to 30 seconds. If a script takes more than 30 seconds, PHP stops the script and returns an error.</p><p>You can change the script run time by changing the max_execution_time directive in the php.ini file.</p><p>When a script is called, the set_time_limit function restarts the timeout counter from zero. It means if the default timer is set to 30 sec, and 20 sec is specified in the function set_time_limit(), then the script will run for 45 seconds. If 0sec is defined in this function, the script takes unlimited time.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">57) What are the different types of errors in PHP?</h3><p>There are three types of errors in PHP.</p><ol class="points"><li><strong>Notices: </strong>These are non-critical errors. These errors are not displayed to the users.</li><li><strong>Warnings: </strong>These are more serious errors, but they do not result in script termination. By default, these errors are displayed to the user.</li><li><strong>Fatal Errors: </strong>These are the most critical errors. These errors may be caused by to immediate termination of the script.</li></ol><hr/><h3 class="h3">58) How to stop the execution of the PHP script?</h3><p>The exit() function is used to stop the execution of the PHP script.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">59) What are the encryption functions in PHP?</h3><p>PHP uses <em>crypt()</em> to hash a string in a one-way format and <em>md5()</em> to produce a 32-character hexadecimal hash from any string.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">60) What is htaccess in PHP?</h3><p>The .htaccess is a configuration file on the Apache server. You can change configuration settings using directives in Apache configuration files like .htaccess and httpd.conf.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">61) Explain PHP explode() function.</h3><p>The explode() function in PHP allows splitting a string into smaller segments delimited by a character. For example, explode(",", "apple, banana, orange") will return an array with the elements ["apple", "banana", "orange"].</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">62) Explain PHP split() function.</h3><p>PHP's split() function uses a regular expression to break a string into an array. Yet, as of PHP 5.3.0, it is no longer supported and is not available in PHP 7.0. You should prefer using preg_split() instead.</p><p><strong>Example using split() (deprecated):</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">$str = "apple,banana,orange";
$result = split(",", $str);
print_r($result);
</textarea></div><p><strong>Recommended alternative using preg_split():</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">$str = "apple,banana,orange";
$result = preg_split("/,/", $str);
print_r($result);
</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">63) How can we get the IP address of a client in PHP?</h3><p>To get the IP address of a client in PHP, you can use the $_SERVER["REMOTE_ADDR"] variable. The PHP $_SERVER variable called "REMOTE_ADDR" will return a client's IP address. The server variable holds the IP address of the user who is currently viewing this page.</p><p><strong>Example</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">$ip = $_SERVER["REMOTE_ADDR"];
echo "Client IP address is: " . $ip;</textarea></div><ul class="points"><li>$_SERVER is an array in PHP that contains details on headers, paths and the locations of scripts.</li><li>"REMOTE_ADDR" holds the IP address that is directly accessing the page.</li></ul><h4 class="n">NOTE: If the user is behind a proxy or load balancer, you may need to check $_SERVER["HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR"] or similar headers to get the real IP address.</h4><hr/><h3 class="h3">64) What is the meaning of a Persistent Cookie?</h3><p>A persistent cookie is permanently stored in a cookie file on the browser's computer. By default, cookies are temporary and are erased if we close the browser.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">65) What is the use of the function 'imagetypes()'?</h3><p>In PHP, using the imagetypes() function provides a bitfield that reflects the GD library support for available image formats. It is used to look at which files (like JPEG, PNG, GIF, etc.) you can produce and alter.</p><p><strong>Example</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">$types = imagetypes();
if ($types &amp; IMG_JPG) {
    echo "JPEG supported&lt;br&gt;";
}
if ($types &amp; IMG_PNG) {
    echo "PNG supported&lt;br&gt;";
}
if ($types &amp; IMG_GIF) {
    echo "GIF supported&lt;br&gt;";
}
</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">66) What are include() and require() functions?</h3><p>The <strong>Include()</strong> function is used to put data of one PHP file into another PHP file. If errors occur, then the include() function produces a warning but does not stop the execution of the script, and it will continue to execute.</p><p>The <strong>require()</strong> function is also used to put data of one PHP file to another PHP file. If there are any errors, then the require() function produces a warning and a fatal error and stops the script.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">67) What is Cookies? How to create cookies in PHP?</h3><p>A cookie is used to identify a user. A cookie is a little record that the server installs on the client's Computer. Each time a similar PC asks for a page with a program, it will send the cookie as well. With PHP, you can both make and recover cookie value.</p><p>You can create a cookie in PHP using the setcookie() function.</p><p><strong>Syntax:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">setcookie(name, value, expire, path, domain, secure, httponly);</textarea></div><p><strong>Where,</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>name:</strong> The name of the cookie.</li><li><strong>value:</strong> The value of the cookie.</li><li><strong>expire:</strong> The expiration time (Unix timestamp).</li><li><strong>path:</strong> The path on the server where the cookie will be available.</li><li><strong>domain:</strong> The domain that the cookie is available to.</li><li><strong>secure:</strong> If true, the cookie will only be transmitted over secure HTTPS connections.</li><li><strong>httponly:</strong> If true, the cookie will be accessible only through HTTP (not JavaScript).</li></ul><p><strong>Example</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">&lt;?php
// Set a cookie that lasts 1 hour
setcookie("username", "JohnDoe", time() + 3600, "/");
// Check if the cookie is set
if (isset($_COOKIE["username"])) {
    echo "Cookie 'username' is set!&lt;br&gt;";
    echo "Value: " . $_COOKIE["username"];
} else {
    echo "Cookie 'username' is not set!";
}
?&gt;
</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">68) What is the Importance of Parser in PHP?</h3><p>PHP parser parses the PHP-developed website from the opening to the closing tag. Tags indicate that from where the PHP code is being started and ended. In other words, opening and closing tags decide the scope of the PHP scripting syntax of closing tags in PHP.</p><p><strong>Syntax of opening tag in PHP:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">&lt;?php</textarea></div><p><strong>Syntax of closing tag in PHP:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="php" name="code">?&gt;</textarea></div><hr/><h3 class="h3">69) How can we create a database using PHP and MySQL?</h3><p>The necessary steps to create a MySQL database using PHP are:</p><ul class="points"><li>Establish a connection to the MySQL server from your PHP script.</li><li>If the connection is successful, write a SQL query to create a database and store it in a string variable.</li><li>Execute the query.</li></ul><hr/><h3 class="h3">70) What is PHP-FPM, and why is it used?</h3><p>PHP-FPM (FastCGI Process Manager) is developed to deal with the demands of a site that gets high traffic. It ensures requests are processed more efficiently by using a group of worker processes and provides process management features for web applications, such as graceful stopping and starting, plus adaptive spawning.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">71) What is the purpose of the isset() and empty() functions in PHP?</h3><ul class="points"><li>isset($var) checks if a variable is set and is not null.</li><li>empty($var) checks whether a variable is empty (i.e., false, 0, '', null, [], etc.).</li><li>They can both be applied to address user input and variable availability when needed.</li></ul><hr/><h3 class="h3">72) What is PDO in PHP, and how is it different from MySQLi?</h3><p>PDO (PHP Data Objects) is a database access abstraction layer that allows access to multiple database types using the same interface. Unlike MySQLi, which works only with MySQL, PDO supports many databases like PostgreSQL, SQLite, etc. It also supports named placeholders and provides better security with prepared statements.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">73) What are magic methods in PHP? Give examples.</h3><p>Magic methods are special functions that start with __ and provide hooks into object behaviour. Examples include:</p><p><strong>__construct()</strong>: Constructor</p><p><strong>__destruct()</strong>: Destructor</p><p><strong>__get()</strong>, <strong>__set()</strong>: Overloading access</p><p><strong>__call()</strong>: Handling undefined method calls</p><p>They allow more dynamic and controlled class behaviour.</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">74) What is the use of the $_SERVER superglobal in PHP?</h3><p>$_SERVER is a PHP superglobal that holds information about headers, paths, and script locations. For example, $_SERVER['HTTP_USER_AGENT'] gives the user's browser info, and $_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] returns the request type (GET, POST, etc.).</p><hr/><h3 class="h3">75) How can you securely handle file uploads in PHP?</h3><p><strong>Secure file uploads involve:</strong></p><ul class="points"><li>Using the $_FILES array to access upload data</li><li>Checking MIME type and file extension</li><li>Setting a size limit</li><li>Using move_uploaded_file() to securely move the file to a desired directory</li><li>Always sanitise the filename and validate user input to prevent security risks.</li></ul><hr/></img></td></tr></tbody></table></div><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="codeigniter-interview-questions-and-answers"><h3 class="h3">Top 40 CodeIgniter Interview Questions (2025)</h3><p class="related-post-content">CodeIgniter Interview Questions A list of top frequently asked CodeIgniter interview questions and answers are given below. 1) What is CodeIgniter? CodeIgniter is an open source and powerful framewor